WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:43.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In a valley in central Slovenia, there's a giant limestone cave called Pekel, which means hell in Slovene. If you look hard at the rocks above the entrance, you can just make out the shape of the devil, which is one explanation for how the cave got its name. That is British expat and 99PI's Slovenia correspondent, Will Aspinall. I recently met up with Andrei Kapla from the Slovenian National Institute of Biology. Andrei studies the ecology of caves and today he is taking me spelunking. I'm not naturally inclined to go into caves.
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马斯。在中斯洛文尼亚的一个山谷里，有一个巨大的石灰岩洞穴叫佩克尔（Pekel），在斯洛文尼亚语中是“地狱”的意思。如果你仔细观察入口上方的岩石，你就能隐约辨认出魔鬼的形状，这也是该洞穴得名的一种解释。那是英国侨民兼 99PI 的斯洛文尼亚通讯员威尔·阿斯皮纳尔（Will Aspinall）。我最近见到了来自斯洛文尼亚国家生物研究所的安德烈·卡普拉（Andrei Kapla）。安德烈研究洞穴生态学，今天他要带我去洞穴探险。我天生就不太喜欢进洞穴。

2
00:00:43.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Is it safe? It's completely safe. But there's one caveat in this case, because it was very, very rainy these days, and hopefully it didn't flood. But if it did flood, then we're in trouble. Okay, we're in the cave. The cave is narrow, only about a metre wide in places, but it's like 15 metres high. It's a chasm, and it's flooded. Oh, it's beautiful.
安全吗？完全安全。但这次有个前提，因为这几天雨下得非常非常大，希望没有被淹。但如果被淹了，那我们就麻烦了。好了，我们进洞了。洞穴很窄，有些地方只有一米左右宽，但大约有15米高。这是一个裂谷，而且被水淹了。哦，太美了。

3
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Yeah, it is, but wet. You aren't scared of caves? Never was. Actually, I'm more comfortable in a cave than with people. Andrei and I are not exploring a cave called Hell just for fun. We're on a mission to catch an elusive insect that Andrei studies. A week earlier he left seven traps inside the cave and baited them with a special recipe. It is a nice mixture of beef liver, fish and gorgonzola. You put them in a vial and marinate them for a few days on sun. Absolutely disgusting. I'm trying to like these creatures.
是的，确实很美，不过很湿。你不怕洞穴吗？从来不怕。其实，我在洞穴里比跟人在一起更自在。我和安德烈探索地狱洞不仅仅是为了好玩。我们负有捕捉安德烈正在研究的一种难寻踪迹的昆虫的任务。一周前，他在洞穴里留下了七个陷阱，并用一个特殊的配方作为诱饵。这是一个由牛肝、鱼和戈贡佐拉奶酪组成的绝妙混合物。你把它们放在一个小瓶里，在阳光下腌制几天。绝对恶心。我正努力去喜欢这些生物。

4
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:46.000
It's easy, they're beautiful. The beautiful creature that we are looking for has only been found in five caves in the north of Slovenia. It's in a group of 51 known beetles called Anophthalmos, Greek for without eyes. These are not very big beetles, nothing special about them. More or less all look the same, so nothing special about the Beatles. Just the name is special. The Beatle is notorious because it is named after, arguably, the worst human being in history. We are hunting for Anophthalmus hitleri, more commonly known as Hitler's beetle.
很简单，它们很美。我们寻找的美丽生物只在斯洛文尼亚北部的五个洞穴中被发现过。它属于51种被称为 Anophthalmos 的已知甲虫群，在希腊语中意为“没有眼睛”。这些甲虫不是很大，没有什么特别之处。大体上长得都一样，所以这些甲虫本身没什么特别的。只有名字很特别。这只甲虫臭名昭著，因为它是以可以说是历史上最糟糕的人命名的。我们正在寻找 Anophthalmus hitleri，通常被称为希特勒甲虫。

5
00:02:46.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Despite living in complete darkness in a handful of caves in a tiny central European country, this unremarkable insect is getting a lot of unwelcome attention right now. Hitler's beetle is at the center of a fierce debate raging in the usually polite worlds of botany and zoology. It's a debate about whether we should rename species that are named after objectionable human beings, and even whether we should be naming organisms after people at all. Plants and animals have no say over what we humans call them. But it turns out a controversial scientific name can profoundly impact how a species fares in the real world.
尽管生活在欧洲一个小国少数几个洞穴的绝对黑暗中，这种毫不起眼的昆虫现在却受到了大量不受欢迎的关注。希特勒甲虫正处于植物学和动物学这两个通常很文明的领域中一场激烈辩论的中心。这是一场关于我们是否应该给以令人反感的历史人物命名的物种重新命名，甚至我们是否应该用人名来命名生物的辩论。植物和动物无法决定我们人类如何称呼它们。但事实证明，一个有争议的学名可能会深刻影响一个物种在现实世界中的命运。

6
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:59.000
The practice of naming species after humans goes back to the 18th century in a man named Carl Linnaeus. Linnaeus was a really interesting character. So he was a Swedish botanist. Sandi Knapp is also a botanist and the author of In the Name of Plants, Remarkable Plants and the Extraordinary People Behind Their Names. He was a Swedish doctor actually, because in the 18th century, which is when Linnaeus lived, medicine was entirely based on plants.
以人类名字命名物种的做法可以追溯到18世纪的一位名叫卡尔·林奈（Carl Linnaeus）的人。林奈是一个非常有趣的人物。他是一位瑞典植物学家。桑迪·纳普（Sandi Knapp）也是一位植物学家，著有《以植物之名：杰出植物及其名字背后的非凡人物》（In the Name of Plants, Remarkable Plants and the Extraordinary People Behind Their Names）一书。实际上他是一名瑞典医生，因为在18世纪林奈生活的年代，医学完全是基于植物的。

7
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:33.000
He was also incredibly arrogant and he was convinced that he could just completely reform how we understood nature. Today his arrogance seems justified because he more or less met that lofty goal. Linnaeus found order in the chaos of the natural world by creating a new framework for naming and categorizing every known animal and plant. Before Linnaeus, the names of organisms were really more like sentences. So it would be a thing like, this is the plant that has white flowers with yellow bits in
他极其傲慢，坚信自己可以彻底改革我们对自然的理解。今天，他的傲慢似乎是合情合理的，因为他或多或少地实现了那个崇高的目标。林奈通过为每个已知的动物和植物创建命名和分类的新框架，在自然界混乱的秩序中找到了条理。在林奈之前，生物的名字实际上更像是句子。所以它会是这样一种东西：这是一种叶子边缘略有缺刻、花朵为白色且中心带有黄色斑点的植物。所以这会是植物的名字。

8
00:04:33.000 --> 00:05:12.000
the middle and leaves which are slightly incised at the edges. So that would be the name of the plant. Linnaeus knew this was really cumbersome, and so he came up with a simpler naming convention. He gave every plant and animal a two-word name, in either Latin or Ancient Greek. To take a well-known example, Linnaeus called human beings Homo sapiens. The first word, Homo, meaning man, is a broad category, the genus. And then sapiens, which means wise or thinking, is the specific name, or the species. Together, the genus and species are combined to create a scientific name that's both unique
林奈知道这真的很笨重，于是他想出了一种更简单的命名惯例。他给每种动植物起了一个由两个词组成的名字，使用拉丁语或古希腊语。举个众所周知的例子，林奈将人类称为智人（Homo sapiens）。第一个词“Homo”意为“人”，是一个广泛的类别，即属（genus）。然后是“sapiens”，意为“明智的”或“有思想的”，是特定的名称，即种（species）。属和种结合在一起，创造出一个既独特又易记的学名。

9
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:54.000
and easy to remember. This became Linnaeus' binomial system, and over time, this concise piece of information design was picked up by scientists all across Europe. And I think it took off because essentially it replicates the noun adjective construction of things that we have in most languages. We have bicycles, which is the genus, and we have red bicycles and green bicycles and golden bicycles and little bicycles. But it wasn't inevitable that Linnaeus' binomial system would become the standard. At the time, there were lots of weird and, frankly, confusing alternatives. My favourite is one that was published in, I don't know, about 1760 by someone called
这就成为了林奈的二名法系统，随着时间的推移，这种简洁的信息设计被全欧洲的科学家所采纳。我认为它之所以流行，主要是因为它复制了我们在大多数语言中都有的“名词-形容词”结构。我们有自行车，这就是属，我们有红自行车、绿自行车、金自行车和小自行车。但林奈的二名法系统成为标准并非不可避免。当时，有很多奇怪且坦白说令人困惑的替代方案。我最喜欢的是大约在1760年左右由法国一个叫贝尔热雷（Bergeret）的人发表的一种方法。

10
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:30.000
Bergeret in France. In Bergeret's system, each letter of a plant's name corresponded to some aspect of its biology. The idea was that the name could communicate important information about the species, but it looked like a jumble of seemingly random letters. The name of the plant was the combination of those letters, so it was B, R, Q, X, W. You know, I just love it when the B, R, Q, X, W's are flowering in the spring. Is that easier to remember than a tropa belladonna for the deadly nightshade? And you can see why Linnaeus' system caught on.
在贝尔热雷的系统中，植物名字的每个字母都对应其生物学的一些方面。这个想法是，名字可以传达关于该物种的重要信息，但它看起来像是一堆看似随机的字母。植物的名字是这些字母的组合，所以它是 B, R, Q, X, W。你知道，我就是喜欢在春天盛开的 B, R, Q, X, W。这比用来指代颠茄的 Atropa belladonna 更容易记住吗？你能理解为什么林奈的系统会流行起来。

11
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:56.000
Linnaeus was also a brilliant teacher, and at Uppsala University just north of Stockholm, he inspired the brightest and bravest young men to go out and explore the natural world. They were called his apostles, but these were his students who basically went out, he sent them out around the world to collect plants in different parts of the world because at that time that was beginning to become possible.
林奈也是一位出色的老师，在斯德哥尔摩正北方的乌普萨拉大学，他激励了最聪明、最勇敢的年轻出去探索自然世界。他们被称为他的门徒，但这些基本上是他的学生，他把他们派往世界各地，在世界不同地区收集植物，因为在当时这开始成为可能。

12
00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:16.000
His apostles traveled the world on dangerous scientific missions to Java and Japan, Venezuela and Vietnam. Many of them lost their lives to tropical diseases, and Linnaeus decided to honor their contributions to science by naming organisms after them. To use Stanley's analogy, not only were there red bicycles and yellow bicycles,
他的门徒们冒着危险的科学任务前往爪子和日本、委内瑞拉和越南。他们中的许多人死于热带疾病，林奈决定以他们的名字命名生物，以表彰他们对科学的贡献。用斯坦利的类比来说，不仅有红自行车和黄自行，现在也有威尔的自行车和罗曼的自行车了。

13
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:46.000
but there were now Will's bicycles and Roman's bicycles too. And it wasn't just his apostles. Linnaeus also named plants after scientists he admired. Take for example the magnolia. Pierre Magnol was a botanist who wrote a flora of the area around Montpellier and was one of the people who sort of first came up with the concept of plants having families, plants being in families. And Linnaeus was a great admirer of magnols, so he named Magnolia from Magnol.
而且不仅仅是他的门徒。林奈还用他钦佩的科学家的名字来命名植物。以木兰（magnolia）为例。皮埃尔·马格诺尔（Pierre Magnol）是一位植物学家，他写了一本关于蒙彼利埃周边地区植物区系的著作，并且是首先提出植物有科（families）、植物属于科这一概念的人之一。林奈非常钦佩马格诺尔，于是他用 Magnol 命名了 Magnolia。

14
00:07:46.000 --> 00:08:16.000
But Linnaeus was clear that not just anyone should be given the honour of having a species or a genus named after them. He even wrote down guidelines about what type of person was acceptable. They weren't rules as in you had to follow them, but they were his rules for how things should be done properly. Things like names should not be used to gain the favour or preserve the memory of saints or of men famous in some other art. So his view was that you should name things for people who have promoted the science of botany.
但林奈明确表示，不应该随便把以自己名字命名物种或属的荣誉给任何人。他甚至写下了关于什么样的人才是可以接受的准则。它们不是必须遵守的规则，而是他关于如何正确行事的准则。诸如：名字不应用来博取圣人或在其他艺术中出名的人的青睐或保存他们的记忆。所以他的观点是，你应该用那些推动了植物学科学发展的人的名字来命名事物。

15
00:08:16.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Linnaeus named 12,000 species in his lifetime, and actually very few of those were named after people. But in the centuries that followed, as more and more plants and animals got discovered, the practice became increasingly common. In Linnaeus' guidelines about what kind of people you should name a species after went more or less out the window. Increasingly, scientists began to name species after people they just happened to be fond of. Slovenia's infamous beetle was very nearly given a different name. The insect was discovered in 1932 by a Slovenian naturalist named Vladimir Khodrich in a cave very close to the one I visited with Andrei.
林奈一生命名了12,000个物种，实际上其中只有极少数是以人名命名的。但在随后的几个世纪里，随着越来越多的动植物被发现，这种做法变得越来越普遍。林奈关于应该以什么样的人命名物种的准则基本上被抛到了九霄云外。科学家们越来越开始用他们恰好喜欢的人的名字来命名物种。斯洛文尼亚臭名昭著的甲虫差点被赋予另一个名字。这只昆虫是1932年由斯洛文尼亚自然学家弗拉基米尔·科德里奇（Vladimir Khodrich）在离我和安德烈参观的洞穴非常近的一个洞穴中发现的。

16
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Khodrich thought he had found a new species, but he wanted a second opinion. And so he sent a specimen to an Austrian beetle collector called Oskar Scheibel. Oskar Scheibel was actually very good entomologist. He had money, so he could afford to go to trips. He could afford to buy specimens from people like Mr. Kodric. And he had vast knowledge about this. Scheibel confirmed that the beetle was a new species.
科德里奇认为他发现了一个新物种，但他想要征求第二种意见。于是他将一个标本寄给了一位名叫奥斯卡·谢贝尔（Oskar Scheibel）的奥地利甲虫收藏家。奥斯卡·谢贝尔实际上是一位非常优秀的昆虫学家。他很有钱，所以他负担得起去旅行。他负担得起从像科德里奇先生这样的人那里购买标本。他对这方面有着渊博的知识。谢贝尔确认这只甲虫是一个新物种。

17
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:57.000
And adhering to Linnaeus's guidelines, he initially agreed to name the new beetle Anophthalmus Kudrychi to honor the discoverer. But then he had a radical change of heart, because in addition to being a world-famous bug collector... Unfortunately, he was a passionate Nazi. And so Scheibel's formal submission of Anaphthalmus Hitleri in a German scientific journal in 1937
为了遵循林奈的准则，他最初同意将这只新甲虫命名为 Anophthalmus kudrychi，以纪念发现者。但随后他的想法发生了彻底的转变，因为除了是一名世界著名的昆虫收藏家……不幸的是，他是一位狂热的纳粹分子。因此，谢贝尔于1937年在一家德国科学期刊上正式发表 Anophthalmus hitleri，将他的一生两大挚爱——昆虫学和阿道夫·希特勒结合在了一起。

18
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:30.000
combined his two great loves, entomology and Adolf Hitler. This part. The gentleman, the Chancellor of the Reich, Adolf Hitler, as an expression of my association to him. So he gives this species to Adolf Hitler as his devotion to him. Hitler was apparently quite tickled by this gesture. According to some sources, he sent a personal letter to Scheibel thanking him for the honor. Naming a species after Hitler might be particularly ill-advised,
这部分。这位绅士，帝国总理阿道夫·希特勒，作为我对他的联系的表达。因此，他将这个物种献给阿道夫·希特勒，以表达对他的忠诚。希特勒显然对这个举动感到很高兴。据一些消息来源称，他给谢贝尔写了一封私人信件，感谢他给予的荣誉。以希特勒的名字命名物种可能特别不明智，但这这种事情一直在发生。

19
00:10:30.000 --> 00:11:02.000
but this kind of thing happens all the time. There are around 2 million known species in the world today. Each year, another 18,000 get discovered. And every new species needs a name. Which has led to some, shall we say, interesting choices? We did so much field work and went to so many different sites that, you know, that those first few months when we first started, we're like, oh, man, look, there's new species. We're really excited. But we just kept finding more and more and more. This is Derek Hennan. He's a diplodologist.
当今世界大约有200万个已知物种。每年又有18,000个被发现。每一个新物种都需要一个名字。这导致了一些，可以说是有趣的选择吗？我们做了那么多的实地考察，去了那么多不同的地方，你知道的，刚开始的头几个月，我们就像，哦，天哪，看，有新物种。我们真的很兴奋。但我们只是不断发现更多更多。这是德里克·海南（Derek Hennan）。他是一位倍足动物学家。

20
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:33.000
That's the person who studies millipedes. He's a diplodologist. That's the person who studies millipedes. In 2021 and 2022, Derek and two colleagues from Virginia Tech published several papers based on years researching the millipedes of Appalachia. Over that time, they discovered 50 new millipede species, and each one needed a name. When you have 50 new species to name, you're really trying to pull from anything to kind of make them unique and also just to sound different. They named some millipedes for the location where they were found, like Nenaria scholastica,
那是研究马陆的人。他是一位倍足动物学家。那是研究马陆的人。在2021年和2022年，德里克和来自弗吉尼亚理工学院的两名同事根据多年研究阿巴拉契亚马陆的成果发表了几篇论文。在那期间，他们发现了50个新的马陆物种，每一个都需要一个名字。当你需要给50个新物种命名时，你真的会试图从任何地方取材，让它们显得独特，同时也听起来与众不同。他们以发现它们的地点来命名一些马陆，比如 Nenaria scholastica，是在弗吉尼亚州华盛顿与李大学校园里发现的。

21
00:11:33.000 --> 00:12:08.000
discovered on the campus of Washington and Lee University in Virginia. Others got names related to how they looked, like Nenaria serpens, a millipede with distinctly snake-like features. But with so many new millipedes, place names and simple adjectives wouldn't suffice. And so they decided to name some of them after people. And if you want an obscure millipede to stand out, why not name it after one of the most famous and beloved people on the planet? Yeah, well, first I would say, you know, I was just a fan of Taylor Swift's music
其他的则得到了与它们外表相关的名字，比如 Nenaria serpens，一种具有明显蛇类特征的马陆。但是面对如此多的新马陆，地名和简单的形容词已经不够用了。因此他们决定用人的名字来命名其中的一些。如果你想让一个晦涩难懂的马陆脱颖而出，为什么不以星球上最著名、最受喜爱的名人之一来命名它呢？是的，好吧，首先我想说，你知道的，我只是泰勒·斯威夫特音乐的粉丝，当我们开车去这些实地考察地点时，你知道的，只要有可能我就会播放泰勒·斯威夫特（Taylor Swift）的歌，所以，你知道的，某种程度上是为了向她的音乐致敬，它在我需要的时候保持了我的好心情。我想以她的名字命名一个物种。

22
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:43.000
and we'd be driving to these field sites and you know, I would like to play Taylor Swift when I could and so, you know, kind of as a nod to her music keeping me in a good mood when I needed it. I wanted to name a species after her. When Derek named a millipede, Nonariaaria swifti after the world's biggest pop star, he became perhaps the first diplodologist to be featured in the pages of Rolling Stone. Which was great because normally millipedes don't make front page news too often, so it was a fun experience. And I was just glad to have people excited about millipedes.
当德里克以这位世界最大流行歌星的名字将一种马陆命名为 Nanaria swifti 时，他或许成为了第一个登上《滚石》杂志页面的倍足动物学家。这太棒了，因为通常马陆不会太频繁地登上头版新闻，所以这是一次有趣的经历。我只是很高兴人们对马陆感到兴奋。

23
